{"authored" [{"question" "q-104134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b48a372790fc37c025de034462efbe04277ae345a801af901d5dcb0a718011b" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ {{field-2}} |  | $-1$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b48a372790fc37c025de034462efbe04277ae345a801af901d5dcb0a718011b" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319395" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | $4$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $7$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b48a372790fc37c025de034462efbe04277ae345a801af901d5dcb0a718011b" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319370" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{4}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-k-6)-(0-3) \\\\  & =-2k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{11}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{11} & =(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-9}\\cdot (-k-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+6}{2k+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{11}^{′}=\\frac{4}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+6}{2k+9} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 5(k+6) & =4(2k+9) \\\\ 5k+30 & =8k+36 \\\\ 3k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b48a372790fc37c025de034462efbe04277ae345a801af901d5dcb0a718011b" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-9"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/state.json" "file_sha256" "121b28f882cf65ac68302e66e6d75e720d229ac42296ae6807a0b7ee44e36eb2" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/example-4514.json" "file_sha256" "ba410b9f602b76425b2ca4aad6e52ff839c3c87ec3f1f8c843054b1623826485" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -(-4) & -3 \\\\ -(14) & -8 & -(-4) \\\\ 3 & -(-2) & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/example-4514.json" "file_sha256" "ba410b9f602b76425b2ca4aad6e52ff839c3c87ec3f1f8c843054b1623826485" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/example-4516.json" "file_sha256" "b1948ce779130df2cc885c1d39d88a5c3e8d3136b4e46ef64013d5f86ba5724d" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/example-4516.json" "file_sha256" "b1948ce779130df2cc885c1d39d88a5c3e8d3136b4e46ef64013d5f86ba5724d" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/example-4515.json" "file_sha256" "868190397d4bc15ef5332195462c8688812877ed15ebbb02c5cd1f81047ca533" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/example-4515.json" "file_sha256" "868190397d4bc15ef5332195462c8688812877ed15ebbb02c5cd1f81047ca533" "basis" 767 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-18281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/state.json" "file_sha256" "52865443a90daa78a12f1ed99b43b88f4face2d0677c6cadb7f57a4bd441760a" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-18281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+9)-4(0-6)+1(0+2) \\\\  & =18+24+2 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{44}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/state.json" "file_sha256" "52865443a90daa78a12f1ed99b43b88f4face2d0677c6cadb7f57a4bd441760a" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-18281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce5bcc711083315f5414ee17801bf085412f4e81564c002496133c9b367a66df" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-18281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/state.json" "file_sha256" "52865443a90daa78a12f1ed99b43b88f4face2d0677c6cadb7f57a4bd441760a" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-18281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}